<div class=\"label\">self-loop(s) each</div><div class=\"bar-container\"><div class=\"bar\" id=\"pBar\" style=\"width:0%;background:#e8d44d\"><span></span></div></div><div class=\"intervals\"><span class=\"interval\">P1 (0)</span><span class=\"interval\">P5 (7)</span></div></div><div class=\"card imperfect\"><h3>Imperfect Consonances</h3><div class=\"count\" id=\"iCount\">0</div><div class=\"label\">self-loops each</div><div class=\"bar-container\"><div class=\"bar\" id=\"iBar\" style=\"width:0%;background:#4fc3f7\"><span></span></div></div><div class=\"intervals\"><span class=\"interval\">m3 (3)</span><span class=\"interval\">M3 (4)</span><span class=\"interval\">m6 (8)</span><span class=\"interval\">M6 (9)</span></div></div></div><div class=\"ratio-box\"><div style=\"color:#888;font-size:0.9em\">Bottleneck Ratio</div><div class=\"big\">1 : 12</div><div style=\"color:#888;font-size:0.9em\">A perfect consonance is 12× more constrained</div></div><div class=\"explanation\"><p>A <b style=\"color:#e8d44d\">self-loop</b> is a voice leading that preserves the current interval. For a self-loop (b, s), we need s = b (both voices move the same amount). At a <b style=\"color:#e8d44d\">perfect</b> consonance, this is <em>parallel motion into a perfect consonance</em> — forbidden unless b = 0 (no motion). At an <b style=\"color:#4fc3f7\">imperfect</b> consonance, parallel motion is unrestricted, so all 12 values of b work.</p><p style=\"margin-top:10px\">This is the mathematical reason why parallel fifths and octaves are forbidden in classical counterpoint: they would require self-loops that don't exist in the quiver.</p></div><script>setTimeout(()=>{document.getElementById('pCount').textContent='1';document.getElementById('iCount').textContent='12';document.getElementById('pBar').style.width=(1/12*100)+'%';document.getElementById('iBar').style.width='100%'},300)</script></body></html>"
