n:width 1s}.bar-imperfect{background:linear-gradient(90deg,#51cf66,#339944);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:600;font-size:.9em;flex:1}.explanation{max-width:600px;margin-top:25px;padding:20px;background:#16163a;border-radius:12px;font-size:.9em;line-height:1.6;color:#aaa}.explanation strong{color:#e0e0e0}.explanation em{color:#a0c4ff;font-style:normal}</style></head><body><div class='title'>The Perfect Consonance Bottleneck</div><div class='subtitle'>Why parallel fifths and octaves are forbidden: a 12:1 self-loop ratio</div><div class='comparison'><div class='card perfect'><h3>Perfect Consonance (e.g., P5)</h3><div class='big-number'>1</div><p>self-loop (identity only)</p><div class='loop-grid' id='perfect-grid'></div></div><div class='card imperfect'><h3>Imperfect Consonance (e.g., M3)</h3><div class='big-number'>12</div><p>self-loops (all motions)</p><div class='loop-grid' id='imperfect-grid'></div></div></div><div class='ratio-bar'><span style='color:#888'>Incoming edges:</span><div class='bar-container'><div class='bar-perfect' style='width:45.9%'>Perfect: 61</div><div class='bar-imperfect'>Imperfect: 72</div></div></div><div class='explanation'><p>A <em>self-loop</em> is a voice leading where both voices move but maintain the same interval. For a <strong>perfect consonance</strong> like the fifth, self-loops require parallel motion — both voices moving by the same amount. But the fundamental rule of counterpoint forbids parallel motion into perfect consonances. So only the <strong>identity</strong> (no motion at all) survives.</p><p style='margin-top:10px'>For an <strong>imperfect consonance</strong> like the major third, parallel motion is unrestricted. All 12 possible parallel motions (one for each semitone amount) are legal self-loops.</p><p style='margin-top:10px'>This <em>12:1 ratio</em> is the categorical manifestation of the centuries-old prohibition on parallel fifths and octaves. Perfect consonances are \"sticky\" — y