lphabet A =</label><select id='A'><option value='2' selected>2</option><option value='3'>3</option><option value='4'>4</option></select><label style='margin-left:15px'>Length L =</label><select id='L'><option value='2'>2</option><option value='3' selected>3</option><option value='4'>4</option></select></div><div class='step'><b>Step 1:</b> The Library has A<sup>L</sup> = <span id='nv'>8</span> volumes. Each is a function assigning symbols to positions.</div><div class='step'><b>Step 2:</b> A \"self-evaluation\" maps volumes to symbols: there are A<sup>A<sup>L</sup></sup> = <span id='nse'>256</span> of these — far more than volumes.</div><div class='step'><b>Step 3:</b> Suppose each volume encodes a self-evaluation (one row of the matrix below). The <span style='color:#c084fc'>diagonal</span> entries are highlighted.</div><div class='matrix-wrap'><table class='matrix' id='mtx'></table></div><div class='step'><b>Step 4:</b> Build the <span style='color:#6ee7b7'>anti-diagonal function</span>: flip each diagonal entry (add 1 mod A). This function <em>differs from every row</em> at the diagonal position.</div><div class='result'><div class='title'>∴ No volume encodes the anti-diagonal function</div><div class='detail' id='conclusion'>The diagonal function disagrees with every encoded self-evaluation at exactly one known position — so it cannot appear as any row in the matrix. No matter how we assign self-evaluations to volumes, at least one self-evaluation is always missed.</div></div><div class='info'>This is a finite version of Cantor's diagonal argument.<br>It connects to Lawvere's fixed point theorem, Gödel's incompleteness,<br>and Turing's halting problem — all instances of the same deep structure.</div></main><script>const $=id=>document.getElementById(id);function update(){const A=+$('A').value,L=+$('L').value;const N=Math.pow(A,L);const nse=Math.pow(A,N);$('nv').textContent=N;$('nse').textContent=nse>1e6?A+'^'+N+' ≈ 10^'+(N*Math.log10(A)).toFixed(0):nse;if(N>16){