lphabet A =</label><select id='A'><option value='2' selected>2</option><option value='3'>3</option><option value='4'>4</option></select><label style='margin-left:15px'>Length L =</label><select id='L'><option value='2'>2</option><option value='3' selected>3</option><option value='4'>4</option></select></div><div class='step'><b>Step 1:</b> The Library has A<sup>L</sup> = <span id='nv'>8</span> volumes. Each is a function assigning symbols to positions.</div><div class='step'><b>Step 2:</b> A \"self-evaluation\" maps volumes to symbols: there are A<sup>A<sup>L</sup></sup> = <span id='nse'>256</span> of these — far more than volumes.</div><div class='step'><b>Step 3:</b> Suppose each volume encodes a self-evaluation (one row of the matrix below). The <span style='color:#c084fc'>diagonal</span> entries are highlighted.</div><div class='matrix-wrap'><table class='matrix' id='mtx'></table></div><div class='step'><b>Step 4:</b> Build the <span style='color:#6ee7b7'>anti-diagonal function</span>: flip each diagonal entry (add 1 mod A). This function <em>differs from every row</em> at the diagonal position.</div><div class='result'><div class='title'>∴ No volume encodes the anti-diagonal function</div><div class='detail' id='conclusion'>The diagonal function disagrees with every encoded self-evaluation at exactly one known position — so it cannot appear as any row in the matrix. No matter how we assign self-evaluations to volumes, at least one self-evaluation is always missed.</div></div><div class='info'>This is a finite version of Cantor's diagonal argument.<br>It connects to Lawvere's fixed point theorem, Gödel's incompleteness,<br>and Turing's halting problem — all instances of the same deep structure.</div></main><script>const $=id=>document.getElementById(id);function update(){const A=+$('A').value,L=+$('L').value;const N=Math.pow(A,L);const nse=Math.pow(A,N);$('nv').textContent=N;$('nse').textContent=nse>1e6?A+'^'+N+' ≈ 10^'+(N*Math.log10(A)).toFixed(0):nse;if(N>16){$('mtx').innerHTML='<tr><td class=\"header\" style=\"padding:20px;color:#c084fc\">Matrix too large to display ('+N+'×'+N+'). Try smaller parameters.</td></tr>';return}const vols=[];for(let i=0;i<N;i++){const v=[];let x=i;for(let j=0;j<L;j++){v.push(x%A);x=Math.floor(x/A)}vols.push(v)}const matrix=[];for(let i=0;i<N;i++){const row=[];for(let j=0;j<N;j++){row.push(vols[i][j%L])}matrix.push(row)}let html='<tr><td class=\"header\"></td>';for(let j=0;j<N;j++)html+='<td class=\"header\">v<sub>'+j+'</sub></td>';html+='<td class=\"header\" style=\"color:#6ee7b7\">diag</td></tr>';const diag=[];for(let i=0;i<N;i++){const d=(matrix[i][i]+1)%A;diag.push(d);html+='<tr><td class=\"header\">f<sub>v'+i+'</sub></td>';for(let j=0;j<N;j++){const cls=i===j?'diag':'normal';html+='<td class=\"'+cls+'\">'+matrix[i][j]+'</td>'}html+='<td class=\"flipped\">'+d+'</td></tr>'}$('mtx').innerHTML=html}['A','L'].forEach(id=>$(id).addEventListener('change',update));update()</script></body></html>"
